00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:02.149 align:start position:0%
 
it's great to welcome Andrew Torres back  to the program today he's a graduate of  Harvard Law who has been practicing law  for 21 years also co-host of the opening  arguments show a legal podcast that  helps you make sense of the news and at  the top of that list for me right now  Andrew is net neutrality net neutrality  an issue that is political it is a legal  issue it is an issue in so many  different ways and I guess to start us  off one question is we know that we knew  when Donald Trump selected Ajit pi to  run the FCC that repealing net  neutrality was going to be a priority  does a Jeep I need to give justification  for the repeal of net neutrality or if  that's what the FCC wants to do can they  just sort of do it well the the answer  to that is it is kind of both so first  David thanks for having me back on the  show I had a lot of fun last time this  is great  second the FCC is going through what  they call a rulemaking set of set of  procedures and so yes they do have to  make findings and the FCC also has to  open up those findings for a period of  public notice and comment which is in  fact you know what they've done so the  the this preliminary decision to reverse  net neutrality was actually made in May  of 2017 it was a two-to-one vote and  over the rather strenuous objection of  Commissioner Clyburn  and then as a result of that vote the  FCC said that they were going to do  really two things number one they were  going to eliminate the 2015 open  Internet order passed by Obama's FCC  that classified Internet service  providers as title to common carriers  and we can talk about what that means in  a minute and then number two they wanted  to open up for comment and the precise  language was to seek comment on whether  to keep modify or eliminate the  bright-line rules that were set forth in  that in that order and then after  the comment period which received as far  as I can tell a record number of  comments for any agency this was it was  highlighted on on John Oliver show and  others and so 22 million people filed  comments I'm guessing that most of those  people did not file comments that said  yes please trash the the the clear  bright line rules but nevertheless from  May to to to the end of November  the FCC moved its position from consider  whether to eliminate those rules to  eliminate those rules and as far as the  comments because I think this is an  interesting thing the FCC needs to be  open to and and accept comments and we  reported earlier in the week about how a  number of the comments over a million of  the comments actually in favor of  getting rid of net neutrality seemed to  have been astroturf or fake comments in  some way shape or form but that aside  just because the FCC has to be open to  receiving comments do they actually need  to go make decisions based on the  consensus from those comments or the  plurality of what the comments want or  do they just need to receive them and  then they can still move however they  want no it's the latter right and that's  why I said the answer to your question  was both so they do have to follow those  procedures but once they admit the  comments they can disregard them which  is which is very clearly what's  happening so as far as title two and the  classification of Internet as common  carriers I think that this is a very  interesting discussion but one that I  feel has a pretty clear answer and in  fact on our Friday show when we answer  questions from the audience someone  wrote in and they said to me hey David  in prota if net neutrality is repealed  in protest  are you willing to cancel your internet  service and if not then why why would  anyone care because you're just gonna  continue paying Comcast for your  internet and that got me thinking I  really only have access to Comcast  Internet where our studio is in Boston  and I need that in order to do my show  so isn't it doesn't that suggest that it  really should be regulated as a utility  it's not a luxury for me to hire Comcast  to provide me the service I need to have  the service in order to do the show  so I think that's a valid argument I  think there are a lot of good arguments  for treating ISPs as title to common  carriers but the legal implication what  happens when you classify something as a  common carrier is you remove it it's  it's it's actually a removal of  jurisdiction from the Federal Trade  Commission and the FTC Act of 1914 which  gives the FTC the power to regulate  quote unfair methods of competition in  or affecting commerce right now  obviously that can be anything but prior  to the 2015 open Internet order there  were lawsuits that was a TC vs. AT&amp;T  that that were brought against AT&amp;T for  deceptive trade practices namely  throttling mobile internet service  right so a a net neutrality type claim  is cognizable under the FTC Act and in  fact that the open Internet order of  2015 resulted in that lawsuit being  mooted and then dismissed in favor of of  AT&amp;T because with ISPs being regulated  as common carriers they were no longer  subject to the jurisdiction of the FTC  so I don't mean to suggest it's a good  thing I I think the arguments for  treating them as a as a title to common  carrier are strong and I think you you  sort of laid that out but I want to  point out that that that's not to me  that's not the real harm of what the FCC  is about to do it's going to make it  more confusing it's going to turn  jurisdiction back over to the FTC the  the FCC is going to take their hands off  but but that doesn't spell the death  knell for being able to use the internet  as as we've come to know it in and of  itself what really hurts I think are the  repeal of the rules so let's talk about  some of those how should you be thinking  through that and you know I every time I  read a statement from an internet  service provider or some of the lobbying  groups that are that are working with  these internet service providers a lot  of times you'll hear them say things  like listen you don't have to worry  because it's not to the advantage of  these ISPs to start either throttling or  creating slow and fast lanes or whatever  they would never do that and I find that  not even worthy of analysis because  shouldn't we be concerned with what  could be done legally it isn't that the  bigger concern than whether we believe  that the current executives of a company  do or don't have an interest in doing it  isn't that what contract law is all  about what could you do yeah so number  one I think the argument isn't a good  argument because we know I mean AT&amp;T  previously excuse me Verizon previously  restricted access to Google Wallet in  ability 2014 so this is this is not just  something that is speculative it's  something that is PS want to do and will  do if they have the power to do so and  the backlash is you know is muted enough  right and and and then secondarily I  think that argument right we can talk  about the throttling and the paid  prioritization the faster and slower  lanes but a key component of the of the  the 2015 open Internet rule was no  interference with edge providers right  and so edge providers are people like  you and I right we are delivering a  service that is carried over the ISP to  end-users that also includes much larger  edge providers like Netflix and and so  forth and and and presently the net  neutrality rules prevent ISPs which own  edge providers from discriminating  against which edge providers they carry  over their lines repealing that that  particular rule I mean I I just can't I  can't come up with a scenario under  which you could convince a company that  it's not in their interest to deny  access to their competitor and so you  know I don't so so so the answer to your  question is yes right we should be  concerned about what is the legal  landscape permit but but then the  further answer is we're not talking  about things that are speculative right  now we are talking about definite  concrete  arms that are you know to the enormous  economic advantage of the ISP in your  understanding of what this process is  that that is being undertaken right now  is there really anything consumers can  do to fight against this I mean we've  talked about battle for the Netcom which  allows you to contact your  representatives and state your  opposition to this repeal verizon  protest calm people will be protesting  because of a gpi's former connection to  to Verizon people are going to be  protesting early in December at Verizon  stores but does any of this stuff really  make a difference can anything be done  to stop this so in terms of stop this  meaning the the FCC taking action on  December 14th to repeal the 2015 open  Internet order I'm sorry to say I don't  think that that can be stopped yeah  there are it's clear the Commission has  the votes the minority commissioners  have been vocal in their opposition  Commissioner Clyburn released a rather  humorous and unprecedented for the FCC  which is not known for their sense of  humor  lexicon of to how to translate the the  the 230 page Internet restoring Internet  freedom order that a GTI put out so  those positions have been staked out  that vote is a foregone conclusion so  then the question is what do we do in  the interim and I think the two things  we do in the interim are number one we  bring economic pressure in the form of  public backlash against the ISPs right  it is not reasonable to say you know you  win when there's a sole provider in your  area that you know you've got a you've  got to cancel your internet service I  mean that that's part of the problem you  laid out but but continuing to sort of  keep this in the public and not have the  the line in the sand be oh we lost on  December 14th we're going to lose on  December 14th but but that doesn't mean  the company still won't make an economic  decision if they think that you know  there's going to be tremendous backlash  and uncertainty  that's not a great it's look that's not  a great line of defense but but it is  kind of step one and then step two is  switch our focus to the FTC to the  Federal Trade Commission and say look if  ISPs are no longer title to common  carriers they are subject to 15 USC 45 a  1 they are subject to the FTC Act they  can be regulated under net neutrality  type rules for deceptive or unfair  methods of competition and these things  which particularly restricting access to  edge provides are unfair methods of  competition whether that's going to get  a better hearing at Trump's FTC than it  does at Trump's FCC you know I mean one  of the things I say on my show all the  time these elections have consequences  and you know this is what we are we're  in a regime that wants to dismantle  everything that looks like a regulation  even if it's a regulation that only  applies to six people right like this  this order really applies to you know  the CEOs of the largest isp  multi-billion dollar mega conglomerates  and that's it but you know we have an  administration and staffing executive  agencies that wants to tear down every  regulation because it's a regulation I  wish we had a less depressing note - and  part one of the interview on but we've  been speaking with Andrew Torres he is  co-host of the opening arguments show  we've been talking about net neutrality  and on tomorrow's program we will pick  it up with him talking about the robert  muller investigation andrew thanks for  joining us  thank you very much

